Multi‑Planetary Human Development (MPHD)

The Human Factors Engineering of Civilization Building

A Self‑Limiting Framework for Cultivating World‑Builders Without Breaking Them

Ouadi Maakour
Revised Theoretical Manuscript
June 2026

---

Abstract

Space settlement will fail not for lack of propulsion but for lack of ordinary humans who can sustain creative, coordinated, and ethical effort across interplanetary timescales without destroying themselves or their crews. This dissertation establishes Multi‑Planetary Human Development (MPHD) as a new branch of Human Factors Engineering. It is not a proposal to select exceptional individuals; it is a framework for designing psychological infrastructure—habitats, AI‑mediated systems, and cognitive skill protocols—that enables ordinary people to rise to the demands of a multi‑planetary future while protecting them from the very drives that make them productive.

The core construct is the Multi‑Planetary Human Index (MPHI), a dynamic, shadow‑aware, neurodivergence‑inclusive index governed by a threshold model. For the first time, every productive capacity is mathematically divided by a Shadow Index that quantifies the destructive potential of unregulated traits. The Shadow Index is no longer an abstract concept; its computation is fully specified through observable linguistic features—a validated shadow lexicon enriched with semantic context, syntactic rigidity indices, and dehumanisation markers—whose false‑positive rates are controlled by a simultaneous‑activation requirement and individualised baselines. The calibration table links each score range to predefined, observable degradations in operational safety, making the model a predictive engineering tool, not an abstract moral judgment.

Crucially, the entire discipline is built on a three‑level architecture:

1. Psychological Infrastructure – the foundational concept that a multi‑planetary civilisation requires deliberately engineered psychological support systems, just as it needs energy grids or life‑support hardware.
2. MPHI – the measurement instrument that operationalises this infrastructure into quantifiable, trackable, and falsifiable human‑system variables.
3. Cognitive Resilience Tools (CRT) – the developmental layer that translates measurement into environmental design, training, and support, accessible to ordinary people by default.

This modularity ensures the survival of the paradigm: even if a specific metric fails, the architecture endures; even if the theoretical underpinnings evolve, the tools remain. The framework is constructed through a Comparative Mechanistic Analysis (CMA) of a diverse set of functional archetypes of civilisation‑scale engineering—not one iconic figure, but a spectrum. The analysis is governed by an explicit “Limitations of Historical Inference” protocol and, critically, a formal sensitivity analysis that tests the robustness of downstream design decisions if the inferred mechanisms prove incorrect. Mechanisms are treated as candidate heuristics, never as confirmed psychological profiles.

Every component of the MPHI is designed to be self‑limiting. An Algorithmic Firewall hard‑codes the separation of psychological data from operational decision‑making through differential privacy guarantees, aggregate hazard flags protected by random dummies, and an off‑planet, rotationally independent psychological support officer. An Anti‑Exploitation Protocol enforces a hard floor on rest, a ceiling on burnout, and an inviolable right to opt out. All interventions are designed as environmental modifications, not individual therapies, and the Baseline Resilience Pathway is actively protected against social devaluation. The Neurodivergence factor is reconceived as a Cognitive Specialisation Profile vector, not a single scalar, to capture the multidimensional nature of neurocognitive styles without imposing deficit narratives.

The ultimate aim is not a few brilliant, broken pioneers but a civilisation of ordinary people who carry the psychological architecture to build, sustain, and govern a new world—without destroying themselves or each other in the process.

