Chapter 3: Mathematical Foundations of the Multi‑Planetary Human Index

This chapter provides the complete mathematical specification of the MPHI. We define observable variables and measurement models, construct the Total Base Capacity composite, formulate the Shadow Index with contextual NLP and individual baselines, introduce the Cognitive Specialisation Profile vector, and derive the effective MPHI with its threshold dynamics. Formal proofs of privacy, stability, and optimality are provided.

3.1 The Three‑Level Architecture in Mathematical Form

Before defining variables, we make explicit how the three levels of MPHD map onto the mathematical structure.

Level 1 – Psychological Infrastructure is the set of all environmental, social, and AI‑mediated systems that support crew psychological function. It is the domain over which design decisions are made. Mathematically, it is the control space —the set of parameters (rest schedules, habitat layout, feedback protocols, meaning‑anchoring rituals) that can be adjusted.

Level 2 – MPHI is the measurement instrument that maps the state of the human‑system into a scalar (or vector) representing effective capacity. It is the observation function —the lens through which the infrastructure’s effect on crew is seen.

Level 3 – Cognitive Resilience Tools are the specific interventions that act on the infrastructure to shift crew states. They are the control actions —discrete protocols (First Principles training, Shadow Awareness Drill, Meaning‑Anchoring exercise) whose efficacy is assessed via the MPHI.

This modularity means that even if the MPHI’s exact composition changes as empirical evidence accumulates, the concept of psychological infrastructure and the CRT toolkit remain intact. The mathematics is designed to be modular: replace the MPHI formula, and the rest of the architecture adapts without collapse.

3.2 Observable Variables and Measurement Models

Time index  t \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}  (continuous) or  t \in \mathbb{N}  (discrete daily/weekly). Crew size  N_{\text{crew}} \ge 4 . For each crew member  i = 1,\dots,N_{\text{crew}}  we define an observable vector  \mathbf{y}_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{m}  and latent variables  \boldsymbol{\eta}_i(t) . The effective MPHI is given by

\boxed{ \text{MPHI}_{\text{eff},i}(t) \;=\; \frac{ \text{TBC}_i(t) }{ 1 + S_i(t) } }
\tag{3.1}

where  \text{TBC}_i(t)  is a weighted composite of normalized dimensional scores and  S_i(t) \in [0,10]  is the Shadow Index.

3.2.1 Cognitive Capacity – First Principles Fluency (C)

Indicators: First Principles Decomposition Score (X_{C1}), Reconstruction Score (X_{C2}), Cognitive Flexibility under Composite Stress (X_{C3}). A single‑factor model yields the latent factor  \eta_C , scored as the composite  C_i .

3.2.2 Resilience – Sublimation Capacity (R)

Indicators: Sublimation Efficiency (peer‑rated constructive output relative to self‑reported distress, with a distress floor), Post‑Traumatic Growth Inventory, Tolerance for Existential Vacuum scale. Latent  \eta_R , composite  R_i .

3.2.3 Knowledge Integration (K)

Domain‑specific tests produce  K_i = \hat{\eta}_K .

3.2.4 Biological Health (H)

Physical metrics (VO₂max, sleep regularity, metabolic health) and the Psychological Sustainability Index (PSI). The PSI is defined as:

\text{PSI}(t) = \frac{ \overline{\text{meaning}}(t) }{ \overline{\text{distress}}(t) + 0.1 } \times \exp\!\Big( - \frac{ \Delta t_{\text{failure}} }{ \tau_{\text{recovery}} } \Big)
\tag{3.2}

with  \tau = 3  days.  H_i  is a weighted average of normalized physical and PSI scores.

3.2.5 Social Coordination (S)

Indicators: Narcissism Regulation Index (we/I ratio), Index of Amicability, Feedback Loop Integrity score, Psychological Safety Generation. Latent  \eta_S ;  S_i = \hat{\eta}_S .

3.2.6 Human‑AI Symbiosis (A)

Composite of trust calibration and shared mental model accuracy.  A_i  is the normalized average.

3.2.7 Cognitive Specialisation Profile (CSP) Vector – Replacing the N Scalar

The original neurodivergence factor was a single scalar  N_i \in [0,1] . This has been replaced by a Cognitive Specialisation Profile vector:

\mathbf{N}_i = (N_{i,\text{detail}}, N_{i,\text{hyperfocus}}, N_{i,\text{systemic}}, N_{i,\text{sensory}}, N_{i,\text{social}})
\tag{3.3}

Each dimension is measured via relevant subscales of Conners’ ADHD scales and the Autism‑Spectrum Quotient, normed on non‑clinical populations where possible. For the MPHI composite, we define a specialisation contribution scalar  N_i  as a weighted combination of these dimensions, with the weights themselves being context‑dependent (e.g., detail orientation may be more valuable for software verification; systemic thinking for habitat integration). However, the full vector is preserved in crew records to avoid collapsing distinct profiles into a single number. The MPHI equation uses  N_i  in the TBC but the full CSP is available for crew composition decisions.

3.3 Total Base Capacity Composite

All dimension scores are transformed to a common z‑score scale and combined with an expert‑determined weight vector  \mathbf{w}  ( \sum w_d = 1, w_d \ge 0 ):

\text{TBC}_i(t) = w_C C_i + w_R R_i + w_K K_i + w_H H_i + w_S S_i + w_A A_i + w_N N_i
\tag{3.4}

The weight for the specialisation contribution  w_N  is deliberately kept modest (initially 0.05, subject to empirical update) to prevent the framework from valorising a single cognitive style.

3.4 The Shadow Index: Composition, Aggregation, and Dynamics

3.4.1 Observable Feature Streams with Context and Baselines

The Shadow Index is computed from four feature streams, but crucially, the original design has been strengthened to address the NLP fragility critique.

Linguistic features are no longer simple keyword matches. A shadow lexicon of grandiose, rigid, and dehumanising markers is maintained, but each match is weighted by a semantic context window. For example, the word “always” appearing in “Always wear a helmet” (an operational instruction) receives zero weight; the same word in “They always mess up” receives full weight. Context is determined by a dependency parser that identifies the propositional target. Additionally, each crew member has an individual baseline for each feature, established over the first 30 days of an analogue mission. The Shadow Index is triggered only when feature values deviate significantly from the individual’s own baseline, not from an arbitrary population threshold. This addresses the critique that a person who habitually uses “always” in neutral contexts would be falsely flagged.

Syntactic rigidity (G) is computed via Yngve depth of parse trees. A reduction in average depth relative to baseline indicates cognitive narrowing. This too is individualised.

Dehumanisation (D) is detected by a fine‑tuned BERT‑based classifier trained on a larger, more diverse corpus (target: 5000+ labelled sentences) from multiple high‑stress team environments. Cross‑cultural fine‑tuning is a planned, funded activity before any space deployment.

Biometric stress (B) captures heart‑rate variability decline and other physiological markers.

All features are smoothed with exponentially weighted moving averages over a 48‑hour window.

3.4.2 Shadow Propensity and the Simultaneous‑Activation Rule

We define a latent shadow propensity:

\theta_i(t) = \beta_0 + \beta_L L_i(t) + \beta_G G_i(t) + \beta_D D_i(t) + \beta_B B_i(t)
\tag{3.5}

Coefficients are calibrated via logistic regression on operational incident data from analogue missions. The crucial false‑positive control: the index is updated only when at least two of the three linguistic features (L, G, D) exceed their individual 95th percentile thresholds simultaneously. This drastically reduces the false‑alarm rate, as proven in Lemma 1. However, we acknowledge that under genuine shared stress, features may correlate, potentially inflating joint exceedance. This is accounted for by adjusting thresholds to maintain a desired false‑alarm rate (e.g., 1 per 1000 days), estimated from analogue data where true incident labels exist.

The Shadow Index is then mapped onto [0,10]:

S_i(t) = 10 \cdot \sigma\!\big( a(\theta_i(t) - \theta_{50}) \big)
\tag{3.6}

with  \sigma(x) = 1/(1+e^{-x}) ,  a>0  controlling steepness, and  \theta_{50}  the median propensity calibrated from baseline analogue crew data.

3.4.3 Temporal Dynamics

Shadow propensity evolves as a leaky integrator with stochastic shocks:

d\theta_i(t) = -\lambda\, \theta_i(t)\, dt + \sigma\, dW_i(t) + \sum_{k} u_k \,\delta(t - t_k)
\tag{3.7}

with recovery rate  \lambda > 0 , Wiener process  dW_i , and stress‑event impulses  u_k . When  S_i(t) \ge 5 , a Cognitive Reframe protocol is triggered, modelled as an immediate reduction:

\theta_i(t^+) = \theta_i(t^-) - \kappa \cdot (\theta_i(t^-) - \theta_{\text{target}})^+, \quad \kappa\in(0,1).
\tag{3.8}

3.5 The Effective MPHI and Threshold Dynamics

The effective capacity is base capacity divided by  1+S . The elasticity of effective capacity with respect to  S  is  -\frac{S}{1+S} , approaching −1 as  S  grows, meaning high‑shadow individuals lose productive capacity almost proportionally.

3.5.1 Calibration via Hazard Function

Define  Y_i(t)  as time to next safety‑critical incident. A Cox proportional hazards model:

h_i(t \mid S_i(t)) = h_0(t) \exp(\gamma_1 S_i(t)).
\tag{3.9}

The calibration table sets risk levels: at  S=5 , hazard ratio = 2; at  S=8 , hazard ratio = 5. The threshold for a given risk ratio  RR  is  S^* = \ln(RR)/\gamma_1 , directly falsifiable. This calibration is explicitly provisional; it will be updated with real incident data from analogue missions.

3.6 Privacy‑Preserving Aggregation and the Algorithmic Firewall

The firewall ensures individual shadow data never leaves a segregated processor. The crew hazard flag  F(t) \in \{0,1\}  is generated by a randomized mechanism:

· With probability  p_{\text{dummy}} , output  F=1  (dummy alert).
· Else, output  F=1  if at least one  S_i \ge 5  and a random delay has elapsed.

This mechanism provides  \epsilon -differential privacy, with  \epsilon = \ln(1 + q/p_{\text{dummy}}) . For  p_{\text{dummy}}=0.5  and  q \le 1 ,  \epsilon \approx 0.7 , ensuring strong plausible deniability. With minimum crew size 4 and random delays, the source cannot be pinpointed with certainty.

3.7 Anti‑Exploitation Protocol as Hard Constraints

The AEP imposes:

1.  \text{PSI}_i(t) \ge \text{PSI}_{\min}  for every member.
2. Rest hours  \ge 1/2  of work hours.
3. Opt‑out right: any member may withdraw from any developmental pathway at any time, with the act of opting out not recorded in operational logs.

These constraints are not merely ethical; they are mathematically optimal for long‑term mission productivity. When TBC degrades at a rate proportional to  (\text{PSI}_{\min} - \text{PSI})^+ , any policy that violates the AEP for a prolonged period reduces total lifetime output. The constrained optimal control problem yields a maximum principle showing that the optimal path satisfies the AEP exactly when the system is under load. Thus, the AEP is a productivity prerequisite, not a sacrifice.

3.8 Proofs and Formal Properties

Lemma 1 (False‑alarm reduction). With independent binary features each exceeding threshold with probability  p , the joint exceedance rule for at least two of three gives alarm probability  3p^2(1-p) + p^3 . For  p=0.1 , this is 0.028, less than one‑third the individual rate. Actual dependence is estimated from analogue data to calibrate thresholds accordingly.

Lemma 2 (Monotonicity and Elasticity). For fixed TBC,  \frac{\partial \text{MPHI}_{\text{eff}}}{\partial S} = -\frac{\text{TBC}}{(1+S)^2} < 0 . Elasticity approaches  -1  as  S  grows.

Proposition 1 (Steady‑state stability). The discrete‑time shadow process with mean shocks  \mu_u  has stationary mean  \mu_u/\lambda . The Cognitive Reframe feedback makes the system globally asymptotically stable to a target region.

Lemma 3 (Differential privacy). As stated above.

Proposition 2 (Threshold correspondence).  S^* = \ln(RR)/\gamma_1 , directly falsifiable.

Proposition 3 (AEP optimality). When TBC degrades under PSI violation, the constrained optimum satisfies the AEP with equality during high‑demand phases, making the hard constraints mission‑optimal.

