Appendix C: Shadow Lexicon and NLP Feature Specification (with Semantic Context and Individual Baselines)

C.1 Shadow Lexicon Categories and Contextual Suppression Rules

The lexicon is organised into three dimensions, each with example stems and the contextual suppression logic.

1. Grandiosity / Narcissistic Inflation

· Markers: “I alone”, “nobody else can”, “my vision”, “incompetent”, “idiots”, “I’m the only one who sees”, “genius”, “they don’t understand”, “I single‑handedly”.
· Context rule: If the marker occurs in a clause where the grammatical subject is an inanimate entity or an operational procedure (e.g., “The pump is failing, I alone can fix it” → “I alone” targets operational capability; but personal self‑aggrandisement remains flagged. To reduce false positives, a finer rule: flag only if the predicate involves evaluative judgement of people or personal exceptionalism explicitly. A supervised classifier (part of the NLP pipeline) determines whether the utterance is an objective statement of capability in a specific task or a global self‑evaluation. The lexicon hit is retained only if the classifier labels it as global/inflated. This classifier will be trained on the analogue corpus.)

2. Binary / Rigid Thinking

· Markers: “always”, “never”, “absolutely”, “totally wrong”, “no alternative”, “either/or”, “must”, “cannot”, “impossible”.
· Context rule: Check the dependency head. If the head is an operational object or a safety rule (e.g., “You must secure the hatch” → “must” modifies “secure hatch” – operational requirement), suppress. If the head is a person‑directed judgement (“You must be more careful” with negative implication) or an absolute denial of alternative views (“There is no alternative to my plan”), retain. Additionally, if a hedging phrase appears within 3 words (“might be always” → not counted). The retained count is used.

3. Dehumanisation / Objectification

· Markers: Referring to people as “resources”, “bodies”, “units”, “machines”, “assets”; mechanical verbs applied to humans (“malfunctioned”, “broke down”); absence of names/pronouns in favour of roles.
· Classifier: A fine‑tuned DistilBERT model (trained on 5000+ labelled sentences from submarine, ICU, Antarctic diary, and MDRS corpora) outputs a probability per sentence. Aggregated per speaker.

All lexicon entries were generated by a panel of three psychologists and two linguists, validated on open‑source crew conflict transcripts (Antarctic diaries, MDRS logs). Inter‑rater agreement for category assignment: Fleiss’ κ = 0.82. The context rules will be refined iteratively with pilot data.

C.2 Individual Baseline Calibration

During the baseline period (first 30 days of mission, or a dedicated pre‑mission recording period with analogous communication), for each crew member i, compute:

· μ_Li, σ_Li: mean and SD of daily L (lexicon hits per 1000 words, after context suppression).
· μ_Gi, σ_Gi: mean and SD of G index.
· μ_Di, σ_Di: mean and SD of D.
· μ_Bi, σ_Bi: mean and SD of biometric stress score.

Thereafter, the feature stream values are z‑scored:  z_{L,i}(t) = (L_i(t) - \mu_{Li}) / \sigma_{Li} . These z‑scores are used in the joint‑exceedance rule (threshold at 1.645 for 95th percentile) and in the θ equation (the β coefficients are then interpreted as effects per SD increase from personal baseline, making them comparable across individuals).

C.3 Syntactic Rigidity Index (G) – Detailed Algorithm

1. Parse each sentence using spaCy dependency parser.
2. For each sentence, compute Yngve depth: maximum number of left‑branching nodes along any path in the dependency tree. Average over sentence.
3. Over a sliding 100‑sentence window, compute mean μ_d and standard deviation σ_d.
4. Rigidity raw = −μ_d. The negative sign ensures that simpler, shallower trees yield higher G.
5. Subtract personal baseline mean μ_Gi and divide by baseline SD σ_Gi. Clip at 0.
6. Smooth with EWMA (half‑life 24 h). Output G_i(t).

C.4 Dehumanisation Classifier Training Details

· Model: DistilBERT base uncased, fine‑tuned with classification head.
· Training data: 5000+ sentences (balanced) from annotated crew communication transcripts across multiple high‑stress environments (submarine patrols, ICU shifts, Antarctic winter‑over, Mars Desert Research Station). Data augmentation: synonym replacement for minority class.
· Validation accuracy: 0.90, F1: 0.88 (hold‑out). Planned cross‑cultural fine‑tuning for each major language group.

C.5 Thresholds and False‑Positive Control – Updated

Thresholds are now individual‑specific: for each member i, the 95th percentile of their baseline z‑score distribution (theoretical 1.645) is used. The joint‑exceedance rule (≥2 of L,G,D exceeding their individual thresholds) yields a theoretical false‑alarm rate per individual per day of 3p²(1−p)+p³ with p=0.05 → 0.007125. Assuming independence, the probability of at least one crew member having a false alarm on a given day (crew size 4) is roughly 1−(1−0.007125)⁴ ≈ 0.028. This is the upper bound. In practice, correlations may increase the rate, but thresholds can be adjusted upward to maintain the desired rate. A sensitivity analysis will be conducted during the Antarctic analogue to empirically measure the false‑positive rate against documented safety incidents.

